Title = {Single-nucleosome imaging unveils that condensins and
   nucleosome-nucleosome interactions differentially constrain chromatin to
   organize mitotic chromosomes},
Journal = {NATURE COMMUNICATIONS},
Year = {2024},
Volume = {15},
Number = {1},
Month = {AUG 21},
Abstract = {For accurate mitotic cell division, replicated chromatin must be
   assembled into chromosomes and faithfully segregated into daughter
   cells. While protein factors like condensin play key roles in this
   process, it is unclear how chromosome assembly proceeds as molecular
   events of nucleosomes in living cells and how condensins act on
   nucleosomes to organize chromosomes. To approach these questions, we
   investigate nucleosome behavior during mitosis of living human cells
   using single-nucleosome tracking, combined with rapid-protein depletion
   technology and computational modeling. Our results show that local
   nucleosome motion becomes increasingly constrained during mitotic
   chromosome assembly, which is functionally distinct from condensed
   apoptotic chromatin. Condensins act as molecular crosslinkers, locally
   constraining nucleosomes to organize chromosomes. Additionally,
   nucleosome-nucleosome interactions via histone tails constrain and
   compact whole chromosomes. Our findings elucidate the physical nature of
   the chromosome assembly process during mitosis.
   Here, the authors capture the mitotic chromosome assembly as molecular
   events of nucleosomes in living human cells, investigating how
   condensins and nucleosome-nucleosome interactions constrain chromatin
   during the assembly process in mitosis.},
